#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Shed the largest agent session before the BOX runs out of memory, and say so over Envoy.

Why this exists: on 2026-10-27 sami-rocqperf wedged. earlyoom logged available memory falling
from 65,039 MiB (28.3%) at 08:45:45Z to 22,521 MiB (9.95%) at 08:48:36Z with swap 0 of 0, acted
at its default 10% floor at 08:48:40Z by SIGTERMing eleven processes it picked by RSS, the
kernel's machine-wide OOM followed at 08:53:24Z (4,157 tasks dumped), and from 09:00Z the box
sat at 95-99% SYSTEM cpu with 0% idle -- direct reclaim with nothing left to reclaim -- until it
was rebooted at 13:56Z. Per-session scopes did not protect it: their limits sum to about four
times the machine's RAM, and most sessions on this box have no scope at all: measured
2026-10-27T14:57Z, `user-1000.slice` held 193 GiB, of which 156 GiB was ONE unlimited login
scope (session-3.scope, 1,463 processes) and only 36 GiB the per-session scopes under
app.slice.

This is the part of the guard that ACTS, and it is the last of three layers. The other two are
configuration the same installer lays down (installers/omp-box-memory-guard.sh):

  1. SWAP on /mnt/nvme, with kswapd watermarks that start reclaim earlier, so the kernel has
     somewhere to put cold anonymous memory instead of spinning in direct reclaim.
  2. memory.max on `user-1000.slice`, the aggregate nobody bounded. It is the hard stop that
     keeps the user's sessions together from ever taking the RAM the system's daemons need: the
     kernel memcg-OOMs INSIDE the slice, never a system service. There is deliberately NO
     memory.high: on a Rocq/Lean build farm the slice legitimately runs cache-heavy and sits on
     any memory.high, charging every allocating task for constant reclaim (114,783 throttle
     events in 29 min when it was tried at 82%). Box-wide reclaim is the kswapd watermarks of
     part 1, which cost no per-task throttle; the graduated step below the hard cap is this
     guard, not a slice throttle.
  3. This guard, which sheds ONE session when the first two are not enough.

What one cycle does, /proc and /sys reads only, no transcript reading and no model call:

  1. Read the box: MemTotal, MemAvailable, swap, /proc/pressure/memory; and the bounded slice:
     memory.current, memory.max, its unreclaimable memory, memory.events.local and memory.pressure.
  2. Attribute memory to agent SESSIONS, using the same attribution as its sibling detector
     scripts/omp-memory-watch (imported from it, not re-implemented):
       * a session in a scope of its OWN (`shims/omp` with OMP_MEMORY_MAX starts one) is the
         scope's unreclaimable memory, anon + shmem + kernel + sock;
       * a session with no scope of its own -- the common shape on this box -- is its `omp`
         process plus every descendant, summed over /proc/<pid>/statm. The process alone
         undercounts: at the 2026-10-27T05:55:14Z kill omp held 14.9 GiB of the 24 GiB that hit
         the ceiling and its tool subprocesses held the rest.
  3. Decide, on MemAvailable as a fraction of MemTotal (every default below is a fraction, so
     the same unit is right on a machine with other RAM):
       WARN  0.25 (62.9 GiB here)  tell the INFRA lead and the three largest sessions, once.
       SHED  0.125 (31.5 GiB here) SIGTERM the largest session, after telling it and the lead;
                                   SIGKILL what is left of it after the grace.
       HARD  0.08 (20.1 GiB here)  no grace: SIGKILL at once, and shed again every cycle.
       CLEAR 0.32 (80.5 GiB here)  one clear message, incident closed.
     and on the SLICE, because MemAvailable counts the slice's reclaimable file cache as free
     and so stays high while the slice fills toward its hard memory.max with unreclaimable
     memory: warn when the slice's unreclaimable headroom to memory.max is under 25 GiB, shed
     when under 12 GiB or when the slice's own memory.events.local shows its hard cap firing. Free swap
     under 25% opens an incident too -- the design's reclaim relief is then spent.
  4. Act on at most one session per cycle and never oftener than --shed-interval, so a box that
     is still falling sheds again rather than killing everything at once.

Measured on this box (earlyoom's own minute-by-minute journal, 1,911 samples over the 32 h to
2026-10-27T14:53Z):

  * MemAvailable p50 123.0 GiB, p10 63.1 GiB, p05 51.7 GiB.
  * Below 64 GiB: 208 samples. Below 32 GiB: 55 samples, every one of them inside a runaway
    episode (05:46, 07:41, 01:27, 01:29, 03:35-04:30 and the 08:44 wedge), none in steady work.
  * Fastest sustained fall 23.0 GiB/min (138.1 -> 22.6 GiB in 302 s on 2026-10-26T07:36:50Z);
    fastest single minute 54.6 GiB/min. From the SHED level, 23 GiB/min leaves 82 s, and a
    cycle plus the grace plus the kill costs about 16 s of it.
  * earlyoom (agent-infra's, untouched by this guard) SIGTERMs at 10% = 24.2 GiB, below SHED, so
    shedding a whole session cleanly comes first. NOTE: a swap file disarms earlyoom's
    AND-of-memory-and-swap test until its -s threshold is raised (theorem-labs/agent-infra
    PR #602); until that lands on a box, earlyoom is not a reliable net there, and this guard
    plus the slice's hard memory.max are the protection.

Alerts go over Envoy to agent sessions only, never to a person: the session being shed, and the
INFRA lead. One WARN per incident, one message per shed, one clear.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import errno
import fcntl
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import signal
import socket
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def _sibling(name: str):
    """Load scripts/omp-memory-watch as a module: one attribution, used by both tools."""
    path = HERE / name
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name.replace("-", "_"), str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


watch = _sibling("omp-memory-watch")

Envoy = watch.Envoy
GIB = watch.GIB
CHIEF_OF_STAFF = watch.CHIEF_OF_STAFF
INFRA_LEAD = watch.INFRA_LEAD
cgroup_of = watch.cgroup_of
cmdline_of = watch.cmdline_of
effective_limit = watch.effective_limit
gib = watch.gib
human = watch.human
iso = watch.iso
log = watch.log
rss_of = watch.rss_of
scope_pressure = watch.scope_pressure
scope_tasks = watch.scope_tasks
session_of = watch.session_of

# The state file lives on the big disk beside omp-memory-watch's, and on a machine that has no
# /mnt/nvme under XDG_STATE_HOME instead, so an incident always has exactly one owner. Both
# are "the service's own"; a run on any OTHER path is a test and may not kill a real session
# (see main()).
_NVME_STATE = Path("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/omp-box-memory-guard/state.json")
DEFAULT_STATE = (
    _NVME_STATE
    if Path("/mnt/nvme").is_dir()
    else Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".local/state")))
    / "omp-box-memory-guard/state.json"
)
PAGE_SIZE = os.sysconf("SC_PAGE_SIZE")


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the box


def meminfo() -> dict[str, int]:
    """MemTotal, MemAvailable, SwapTotal and SwapFree in bytes."""
    wanted = {"MemTotal", "MemFree", "MemAvailable", "SwapTotal", "SwapFree", "Dirty"}
    out: dict[str, int] = {}
    for line in Path("/proc/meminfo").read_text().splitlines():
        key, _, rest = line.partition(":")
        if key in wanted:
            out[key] = int(rest.split()[0]) * 1024
    return out


def pressure() -> dict[str, float]:
    """/proc/pressure/memory as {some_avg10, some_avg60, full_avg10, full_avg60, ...}.

    `full` is the share of wall time in which EVERY runnable task was stalled on memory: the
    number that was pinned high while the box sat at 0% idle in reclaim.
    """
    out: dict[str, float] = {}
    try:
        for line in Path("/proc/pressure/memory").read_text().splitlines():
            parts = line.split()
            kind = parts[0]
            for field in parts[1:]:
                name, _, value = field.partition("=")
                if name.startswith("avg"):
                    out[f"{kind}_{name}"] = float(value)
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return out


def psi_text(psi: dict[str, float]) -> str:
    return (
        f"some {psi.get('some_avg10', 0):.1f}/{psi.get('some_avg60', 0):.1f}%,"
        f" full {psi.get('full_avg10', 0):.1f}/{psi.get('full_avg60', 0):.1f}% (avg10/avg60)"
    )


def pressure_of(path: Path) -> dict[str, float]:
    """A cgroup's own memory.pressure, in the same shape as the box's."""
    out: dict[str, float] = {}
    try:
        for line in (path / "memory.pressure").read_text().splitlines():
            parts = line.split()
            for field in parts[1:]:
                name, _, value = field.partition("=")
                if name.startswith("avg"):
                    out[f"{parts[0]}_{name}"] = float(value)
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return out


def slice_state(name: str) -> dict:
    """The bounded slice: what it holds, its memory.max, its UNRECLAIMABLE memory, its events.

    This is the aggregate that was unbounded when the box died: measured 2026-10-27T14:57Z,
    `user-1000.slice` held 193 GiB, of which 156 GiB was the single unlimited login scope
    `session-3.scope` (1,463 processes) and only 36 GiB the per-session scopes under
    `app.slice`. It carries a hard `memory.max` (installers/omp-box-memory-guard.sh), and the
    kernel memcg-OOMs INSIDE the slice when its UNRECLAIMABLE memory (anon + shmem + kernel +
    sock -- the part reclaim and swap cannot give back) approaches that ceiling. memory.current
    does not predict that OOM: it is mostly page cache the kernel drops for free, so it sits near
    the ceiling in ordinary cache-heavy build work while the slice is nowhere near OOM. So the
    signal is the slice's own unreclaimable headroom, and the `memory.events.local` max/oom_kill
    counters, which say the hard cap has actually bitten.
    """
    path = watch.CGROUP_ROOT / name.strip("/")
    state = {"name": name, "present": path.is_dir()}
    if not state["present"]:
        return state
    unreclaimable, current, _fields = watch.scope_pressure(name)
    state["current"] = current
    state["unreclaimable"] = unreclaimable
    state["max"] = watch.read_int(path / "memory.max")
    state["headroom"] = (state["max"] - unreclaimable) if state["max"] else None
    # memory.events.local, NEVER memory.events: the plain file aggregates child cgroups, so its
    # `max`/`oom_kill` count the per-session scopes hitting THEIR own 8-24 GiB caps (2750 of them
    # here), which says nothing about the slice's own hard cap. The `.local` file is the slice's
    # own, and reads max=0 oom_kill=0 when only children have been capped.
    events: dict[str, int] = {}
    try:
        for line in (path / "memory.events.local").read_text().splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
            events[key] = int(value)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    state["events"] = events
    state["psi"] = pressure_of(path)
    return state


def slice_text(state: dict) -> str:
    if not state.get("present"):
        return f"Slice {state['name']}: not on this machine."
    ceiling = state.get("max")
    headroom = state.get("headroom")
    return (
        f"Slice {state['name']}: {gib(state['current'])} held,"
        f" {gib(state['unreclaimable'])} of it unreclaimable (anon+shmem+kernel+sock),"
        f" memory.max {gib(ceiling) if ceiling else 'unset'}"
        + (f", {gib(headroom)} of unreclaimable headroom to it" if headroom is not None else "")
        + f", own events (memory.events.local) high={state['events'].get('high', 0)}"
        f" max={state['events'].get('max', 0)} oom_kill={state['events'].get('oom_kill', 0)},"
        f" its own pressure {psi_text(state.get('psi') or {})}."
    )


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the sessions


def process_table(comm: str) -> tuple[dict[int, int], dict[int, int], list[int]]:
    """One pass over /proc: {pid: ppid}, {pid: rss bytes}, and the pids whose comm matches."""
    parents: dict[int, int] = {}
    resident: dict[int, int] = {}
    candidates: list[int] = []
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        try:
            # /proc/<pid>/stat in one read: ppid is the field after the comm in parentheses,
            # and statm's second field is the resident page count.
            data = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text()
            tail = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()
            parents[pid] = int(tail[1])
            name = data[data.find("(") + 1 : data.rfind(")")]
            resident[pid] = int(Path(f"/proc/{pid}/statm").read_text().split()[1]) * PAGE_SIZE
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
        if name == comm:
            candidates.append(pid)
    return parents, resident, candidates


def descendants(root: int, children: dict[int, list[int]]) -> list[int]:
    found = [root]
    stack = [root]
    while stack:
        for child in children.get(stack.pop(), ()):
            found.append(child)
            stack.append(child)
    return found


def tree_pss(pids: list[int]) -> int:
    """Proportional set size of a set of pids, summed from /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup (bytes).

    Pss charges each shared page to its sharers in equal parts, so summing it over a process
    tree counts a page the tree shares once, not once per process -- the memory that actually
    comes back when the tree is killed. A process that ends mid-read is skipped, not guessed.
    """
    total = 0
    for pid in pids:
        try:
            for line in Path(f"/proc/{pid}/smaps_rollup").read_text().splitlines():
                if line.startswith("Pss:"):
                    total += int(line.split()[1]) * 1024
                    break
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
    return total


class Session(dict):
    """One agent session: what it is, how much memory it holds, and how to shed it."""

    @property
    def key(self) -> str:
        return self["key"]


def sessions(comm: str, guard_pid: int, refine: int = 6) -> list[Session]:
    """Every agent session on this machine, largest first.

    The attribution is omp-memory-watch's, in its two shapes. A session whose `omp` process is a
    descendant of another session's is not its own session: the ancestor owns that memory.
    """
    parents, resident, candidates = process_table(comm)
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    for pid, parent in parents.items():
        children.setdefault(parent, []).append(pid)

    candidate_set = set(candidates)
    guard_line = set(descendants(guard_pid, children))
    found: list[Session] = []
    by_scope: dict[str, Session] = {}
    for pid in candidates:
        # A candidate under another candidate is that session's own child process.
        walker, nested = parents.get(pid, 0), False
        while walker > 1:
            if walker in candidate_set:
                nested = True
                break
            walker = parents.get(walker, 0)
        if nested:
            continue
        cgroup = cgroup_of(pid)
        if not cgroup:
            continue
        limit, limit_from, limited = effective_limit(cgroup)
        tree = descendants(pid, children)
        if limited:
            # Its own scope: exactly what the memcg OOM killer measures, tool subprocesses in.
            scope = cgroup.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
            usage, current, _ = scope_pressure(cgroup)
            session = Session(
                key=scope, kind="scope", cgroup=cgroup, scope=scope, pid=pid, usage=usage,
                current=current, limit=limit, limit_from=limit_from, members=scope_tasks(cgroup),
                measured="anon+shmem+kernel+sock of its own scope",
            )
            # Several omp processes in one scope are one session's lanes on one budget.
            if scope in by_scope:
                if rss_of(by_scope[scope]["pid"]) < rss_of(pid):
                    by_scope[scope]["pid"] = pid
                continue
            by_scope[scope] = session
        else:
            # No scope of its own: the login scope it sits in is shared with every other
            # session in the login, so the session is its process tree.
            session = Session(
                key=f"pid:{pid}", kind="tree", cgroup=cgroup, scope=cgroup.rsplit("/", 1)[-1],
                pid=pid, usage=sum(resident.get(p, 0) for p in tree), current=0, limit=limit,
                limit_from=limit_from, members=tree,
                measured=f"summed RSS of the omp process and its {len(tree) - 1} descendants",
            )
        session["tree"] = tree
        session["is_guard"] = pid in guard_line or guard_pid in session["members"]
        session["started"] = watch.pid_start(pid)
        found.append(session)
    found.sort(key=lambda s: s["usage"], reverse=True)
    # Summed statm RSS double-counts pages a tree shares (its shared libraries, and the
    # copy-on-write pages a fork has not yet touched), so it over-ranks a many-process tree
    # against a scope. For the top few only -- the ones a shed could pick -- re-measure each tree
    # on what killing it would actually free: the proportional set size (Pss) summed from each
    # member's /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup, which charges a shared page to its sharers in equal
    # parts. smaps_rollup walks the VMAs, so it is read for a handful of candidates, not all.
    for session in found[:refine]:
        if session["kind"] == "tree":
            pss = tree_pss(session["members"])
            if pss:
                session["usage"] = pss
                session["measured"] = (
                    f"proportional set size (Pss) of the omp process and its"
                    f" {len(session['members']) - 1} descendants"
                )
    found.sort(key=lambda s: s["usage"], reverse=True)
    return found


def alive(pid: int, started: int) -> bool:
    """Is that exact process still running? pids wrap inside a day on this box."""
    try:
        return watch.pid_start(pid) == started and started != 0
    except OSError:
        return False


def signal_session(session: Session, sig: int) -> int:
    """Signal a session. A scope is signalled through its cgroup, a tree through its pids."""
    sent = 0
    if session["kind"] == "scope" and sig == signal.SIGKILL:
        # cgroup.kill is atomic: every task, including ones forked while we were deciding.
        try:
            (watch.CGROUP_ROOT / session["cgroup"].strip("/") / "cgroup.kill").write_text("1")
            return len(session["members"])
        except OSError:
            pass
    members = scope_tasks(session["cgroup"]) if session["kind"] == "scope" else session["members"]
    # The session leader first, so it can take its own children down on SIGTERM.
    for pid in sorted(set(members), key=lambda p: p != session["pid"]):
        try:
            os.kill(pid, sig)
            sent += 1
        except OSError as error:
            if error.errno not in (errno.ESRCH, errno.EPERM):
                raise
    return sent


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the state


class State(object):
    """The one file this guard owns, held under an exclusive flock for the whole run.

    A second guard (a hand run against the live file) loses the lock and exits rather than
    deciding on a copy of the same incident -- the failure mode that sent one crossing's first
    alert twice on 2026-10-27.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path) -> None:
        self.path = path
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.handle = open(path, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            self.locked = True
        except OSError:
            self.locked = False
            self.data = {}
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        try:
            self.data = json.loads(self.handle.read() or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            self.data = {}

    def commit(self) -> None:
        self.handle.seek(0)
        self.handle.truncate()
        json.dump(self.data, self.handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        self.handle.flush()
        os.fsync(self.handle.fileno())


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the words


def box_line(r: dict) -> str:
    info, psi, levels = r["info"], r["psi"], r["levels"]
    total = info["MemTotal"]
    available = info["MemAvailable"]
    if info.get("SwapTotal"):
        used = info["SwapTotal"] - info.get("SwapFree", 0)
        swap = (
            f" Swap {gib(used)} used of {gib(info['SwapTotal'])}"
            f" ({100 * used / info['SwapTotal']:.0f}%)."
        )
    else:
        swap = " Swap: none on this box."
    return (
        f"Box {socket.gethostname()}: MemAvailable {gib(available)} of {gib(total)}"
        f" ({100 * available / total:.1f}%)."
        f"{swap} Memory pressure {psi_text(psi)}."
        f" Levels: warn {gib(levels['warn'])}, shed {gib(levels['shed'])},"
        f" no-grace {gib(levels['hard'])}, clear {gib(levels['clear'])}."
        f"\n{slice_text(r['slice'])}"
    )


def biggest_line(ranked: list[Session], limit: int = 3) -> str:
    parts = []
    for session in ranked[:limit]:
        who = session.get("session_id") or f"pid {session['pid']}"
        parts.append(f"{who} ({session['key']}) {gib(session['usage'])}")
    return "; ".join(parts) if parts else "none found"


def warn_text(r: dict, ranked: list[Session], reason: str) -> str:
    return "\n".join([
        f"BOX MEMORY LOW on {socket.gethostname()} ({reason}). No session has been shed yet.",
        box_line(r),
        f"Largest agent sessions now: {biggest_line(ranked)}.",
        "DO, now, before this guard decides for you: stop spawning lanes; write your handover"
        " files; shed or finish the lanes you can; restart a session that has been up for hours"
        f" with `omp --resume <session id>`. If MemAvailable reaches {gib(r['levels']['shed'])}"
        " -- or the slice's unreclaimable headroom to its memory.max runs low -- this guard"
        " SIGTERMs the largest session on the box, which may be yours, and SIGKILLs what is left"
        " of it after the grace.",
        "Evidence: /proc/meminfo MemAvailable, free swap, /proc/pressure/memory, the slice's own"
        " memory.current, unreclaimable memory, memory.max and memory.events.local, and per-session"
        " memory from each session's own cgroup (scoped sessions) or its process tree (unscoped).",
        "From omp-box-memory-guard (~/.dotfiles/scripts/omp-box-memory-guard), a systemd --user"
        f" service on {socket.gethostname()}.",
    ])


def shed_text(r: dict, ranked: list[Session], victim: Session, grace: float, hard: bool) -> str:
    who = victim.get("session_id") or ""
    naming = (
        f"session {who}" if who else
        f"an unidentified session (omp pid {victim['pid']}: no session id on its command line"
        " and no session transcript open)"
    )
    how = (
        "SIGKILL at once: the box is below the no-grace level" if hard else
        f"SIGTERM now, SIGKILL of whatever is left in {grace:.0f} s"
    )
    return "\n".join([
        f"SHEDDING THE LARGEST AGENT SESSION on {socket.gethostname()}: {naming},"
        f" {gib(victim['usage'])} ({victim['measured']}).",
        box_line(r),
        f"Why this one: it is the largest agent session on the box. Ranked: {biggest_line(ranked)}.",
        f"How: {how}. Its memory comes back to the box when it exits.",
        "If this is you: your work is NOT saved. Resume with `omp --resume <your session id>`"
        " once the box is back above the clear level; your transcript is on disk, the in-flight"
        " lanes are not. Then come back under a memory ceiling:"
        " `OMP_MEMORY_MAX=24G omp --resume <id>` runs the session in a scope of its own, so the"
        " next time it is the session that dies and not the box.",
        "This guard sheds one session per cycle and no oftener than its shed interval, so a box"
        " that is still falling sheds again rather than killing everything at once. Below it,"
        " the slice's memory.max and agent-infra's earlyoom are the backstops.",
        "From omp-box-memory-guard (~/.dotfiles/scripts/omp-box-memory-guard) on "
        f"{socket.gethostname()}.",
    ])


def clear_text(r: dict, incident: dict, now: float) -> str:
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or now
    sheds = incident.get("sheds") or []
    what = (
        "Sessions shed in this incident: "
        + "; ".join(f"{s.get('session_id') or s['key']} ({gib(s['usage'])})" for s in sheds)
        if sheds
        else "Nothing was shed: the box recovered on its own."
    )
    return "\n".join([
        f"BOX MEMORY CLEARED on {socket.gethostname()}: back above {gib(r['levels']['clear'])}.",
        box_line(r),
        f"The incident opened at {iso(opened)}, cleared at {iso(now)}, lasted"
        f" {human(now - opened)}, and bottomed out at {gib(incident.get('low') or 0)} available.",
        what,
        "No action is needed. From omp-box-memory-guard on " + socket.gethostname() + ".",
    ])


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- the cycle


class Guard(object):
    def __init__(self, options: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
        self.options = options
        self.envoy = None if options.dry_run else Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout)
        self.state = State(options.state)
        self.guard_pid = os.getpid()
        self.last_heartbeat = 0.0

    def send(self, targets: list[str], text: str) -> list[str]:
        told = []
        for target in dict.fromkeys(t for t in targets if t):
            if self.options.dry_run or (self.envoy and self.envoy.send(target, text)):
                told.append(target)
        if self.options.print_alert:
            print(text)
        return told

    def levels(self, total: int) -> dict[str, int]:
        return {
            "warn": int(total * self.options.warn),
            "shed": int(total * self.options.shed),
            "hard": int(total * self.options.hard),
            "clear": int(total * self.options.clear),
        }

    def cycle(self) -> None:
        now = time.time()
        info = meminfo()
        psi = pressure()
        total, available = info["MemTotal"], info["MemAvailable"]
        reading = {
            "info": info, "psi": psi, "levels": self.levels(total),
            "slice": slice_state(self.options.slice),
        }
        levels = reading["levels"]
        bounded = reading["slice"]
        data = self.state.data
        incident: dict | None = data.get("incident")

        # Free swap matters twice. It is the relief the design leans on -- swap lets the kernel
        # reclaim anonymous memory for everything that is not a capped session -- so a nearly
        # full swap file means that relief is spent. It is also where agent-infra's earlyoom
        # comes back within reach: its -s threshold (100% once PR #602 lands, so always armed on
        # the memory side) aside, a full swap file is the last warning before the kernel's own
        # OOM. So < 25% free opens an incident.
        swap_total = info.get("SwapTotal", 0)
        swap_free = info.get("SwapFree", 0)
        swap_frac = (swap_free / swap_total) if swap_total else 1.0
        swap_low = swap_total and swap_frac < self.options.swap_warn

        # The slice's own headroom is the trigger the box-level numbers cannot be, because
        # MemAvailable counts the slice's tens of GiB of reclaimable file cache as available, so
        # it stays high while the slice fills toward its hard memory.max with UNRECLAIMABLE
        # memory and the kernel memcg-OOMs a session inside it with no warning. The slice's
        # unreclaimable headroom to memory.max predicts that OOM; a RISE in its memory.events.local
        # oom_kill or max counter says the hard cap has actually bitten SINCE the last cycle. The
        # counters are cumulative and nonzero from the box's history, so the first cycle only
        # records a baseline -- a rise is measured against a cycle we have seen, never against 0.
        head = bounded.get("headroom")
        last_events = data.get("slice_events")
        events = bounded.get("events") or {}
        now_events = {"max": events.get("max", 0), "oom_kill": events.get("oom_kill", 0)}
        have_baseline = bool(bounded.get("present")) and last_events is not None
        # Two distinct own-cap signals, each a RISE since the last cycle, never an absolute value:
        # oom_killed is the slice's own memory.max actually killing a process; cap_hit is its own
        # max boundary being reached and reclaimed from. Both warrant a shed; only the first is
        # "OOM-killing" in the alert.
        oom_killed = have_baseline and now_events["oom_kill"] > last_events.get("oom_kill", 0)
        cap_hit = have_baseline and now_events["max"] > last_events.get("max", 0)
        if bounded.get("present"):
            data["slice_events"] = now_events
        slice_warn = head is not None and head < self.options.slice_warn * GIB
        slice_shed = oom_killed or cap_hit or (head is not None and head < self.options.slice_shed * GIB)

        box_warn = available < levels["warn"]
        box_shed = available < levels["shed"]
        hard = available < levels["hard"]
        trouble = box_warn or slice_warn or slice_shed or swap_low

        if available >= levels["clear"] and not trouble:
            if incident:
                if incident.get("alerted"):
                    self.send(incident.get("told") or [INFRA_LEAD], clear_text(reading, incident, now))
                log(f"incident cleared: {gib(available)} available, above {gib(levels['clear'])}")
                data.pop("incident", None)
                self.state.commit()
            self.heartbeat(now, reading)
            self.state.commit()
            return

        if not trouble:
            # Between warn and clear with an incident open: it stays open (hysteresis), and
            # with none open there is nothing to say yet.
            self.heartbeat(now, reading)
            self.state.commit()
            return

        ranked = self.rank()
        if incident is None:
            if box_shed:
                reason = f"MemAvailable {gib(available)} is under the shed level"
            elif slice_shed:
                if oom_killed:
                    reason = (
                        f"{bounded['name']}'s own memory.max ({gib(bounded['max'])}) has started"
                        f" OOM-killing (oom_kill rose to {now_events['oom_kill']});"
                        f" {gib(bounded.get('unreclaimable') or 0)} unreclaimable,"
                        f" {gib(head or 0)} of headroom"
                    )
                elif cap_hit:
                    reason = (
                        f"{bounded['name']} has reached its own memory.max ({gib(bounded['max'])});"
                        f" {gib(bounded.get('unreclaimable') or 0)} unreclaimable,"
                        f" {gib(head or 0)} of headroom"
                    )
                else:
                    reason = (
                        f"{bounded['name']} is holding {gib(bounded.get('unreclaimable') or 0)}"
                        f" unreclaimable, only {gib(head or 0)} of headroom to its memory.max"
                        f" ({gib(bounded['max'])})"
                    )
            elif box_warn:
                reason = f"MemAvailable {gib(available)} is under the warn level"
            elif slice_warn:
                reason = (
                    f"{bounded['name']} has {gib(head or 0)} of unreclaimable headroom left to its"
                    f" memory.max ({gib(bounded['max'])})"
                )
            else:
                reason = f"free swap is {gib(swap_free)} of {gib(swap_total)} ({100 * swap_frac:.0f}%)"
            incident = data["incident"] = {
                "opened_at": now, "low": available, "reason": reason, "sheds": [], "told": [],
            }
        incident["low"] = min(incident.get("low", available), available)

        if not incident.get("alerted"):
            targets = [INFRA_LEAD] + [s.get("session_id") for s in ranked[:3]]
            incident["alerted"] = now
            incident["told"] = [t for t in targets if t]
            self.state.commit()
            incident["told"] = self.send(targets, warn_text(reading, ranked, incident["reason"])) or incident["told"]
            log(f"WARN: {incident['reason']}; told {len(incident['told'])} session(s)")

        self.finish_pending(now, reading)
        if box_shed or slice_shed or hard:
            self.shed(now, reading, ranked)
        self.state.commit()
        self.heartbeat(now, reading, force=True)

    def rank(self) -> list[Session]:
        ranked = sessions(self.options.session_comm, self.guard_pid)
        for session in ranked[:4]:
            session["session_id"] = session_of(session["pid"])
        return ranked

    def finish_pending(self, now: float, r: dict) -> None:
        """SIGKILL what is left of a session we SIGTERMed, once its grace is up."""
        pending = self.state.data.get("pending")
        if not pending:
            return
        session = Session(pending["session"])
        hard = r["info"]["MemAvailable"] < r["levels"]["hard"]
        if not alive(session["pid"], session["started"]):
            log(f"shed complete: {session['key']} is gone")
            self.state.data.pop("pending", None)
            return
        if now - pending["termed_at"] < self.options.grace and not hard:
            return
        killed = signal_session(session, signal.SIGKILL)
        log(f"shed escalated: SIGKILL to {killed} process(es) of {session['key']}")
        self.state.data.pop("pending", None)
        # A SIGKILLed tree takes a moment to die and for its pages to return to the box.
        # Hold the next shed for one cooldown so MemAvailable reflects this kill before the
        # guard decides whether another session has to go -- otherwise one spike sheds a
        # cascade of sessions that were never the problem.
        self.state.data["cooldown_until"] = now + self.options.cooldown

    def shed(self, now: float, r: dict, ranked: list[Session]) -> None:
        if self.state.data.get("pending"):
            return
        hard = r["info"]["MemAvailable"] < r["levels"]["hard"]
        # The cooldown and the shed interval both yield to the no-grace level: below it the box
        # is in the kernel's hands and one-per-interval is too slow.
        cooldown_until = self.state.data.get("cooldown_until") or 0
        if now < cooldown_until and not hard:
            return
        last = self.state.data.get("last_shed_at") or 0
        if now - last < self.options.shed_interval and not hard:
            return
        protected = set(self.options.protect)
        candidates = []
        for session in ranked:
            if session["is_guard"]:
                continue
            if session["usage"] < self.options.min_victim * GIB:
                continue
            if not session["cgroup"].startswith("/user.slice"):
                # Nothing outside the user's own slice is an agent session.
                continue
            if self.options.test_only and self.options.session_comm == "omp":
                continue
            session.setdefault("session_id", session_of(session["pid"]))
            candidates.append(session)
        # A coordinator or ORCHESTRATOR session is spared while any other session can be shed:
        # losing the one directing the others costs more than one lane's memory. It is shed only
        # when it is the last candidate -- a leak in it still cannot be allowed to take the box.
        unprotected = [s for s in candidates if s.get("session_id") not in protected]
        victim = (unprotected or candidates)[0] if candidates else None
        if victim is None:
            log(
                f"over the shed level ({gib(r['info']['MemAvailable'])} available) but no"
                f" sheddable agent session: {len(ranked)} candidate(s) with comm"
                f" {self.options.session_comm!r}"
            )
            return
        spared = victim.get("session_id") in protected
        text = shed_text(r, ranked, victim, self.options.grace, hard)
        record = {
            "key": victim["key"], "pid": victim["pid"], "usage": victim["usage"],
            "session_id": victim.get("session_id"), "at": now, "hard": hard,
        }
        self.state.data["last_shed_at"] = now
        self.state.data.setdefault("incident", {}).setdefault("sheds", []).append(record)
        if not hard:
            self.state.data["pending"] = {"session": dict(victim), "termed_at": now}
        self.state.commit()
        told = self.send([victim.get("session_id"), INFRA_LEAD], text)
        if self.options.dry_run:
            log(f"DRY RUN: would shed {victim['key']} ({gib(victim['usage'])}); told {len(told)}")
            self.state.data.pop("pending", None)
            return
        signalled = signal_session(victim, signal.SIGKILL if hard else signal.SIGTERM)
        log(
            f"SHED {victim['key']} ({gib(victim['usage'])}, session"
            f" {victim.get('session_id') or 'unknown'}"
            + (", a protected session shed as the last candidate" if spared else "")
            + f"): {'SIGKILL' if hard else 'SIGTERM'} to {signalled} process(es); told {len(told)}"
        )

    def heartbeat(self, now: float, r: dict, force: bool = False) -> None:
        if not force and now - self.last_heartbeat < self.options.heartbeat:
            return
        self.last_heartbeat = now
        log(box_line(r))

    def run(self) -> int:
        if not self.state.locked:
            log(f"another run holds {self.options.state}; exiting")
            return 0
        log(
            f"omp-box-memory-guard starting: every {self.options.interval:g}s,"
            f" sessions are processes named {self.options.session_comm!r},"
            f" state {self.options.state}" + (" [DRY RUN]" if self.options.dry_run else "")
        )
        stopping = {"now": False}

        def stop(signum, _frame):
            stopping["now"] = True
            log(f"signal {signum}: stopping")

        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, stop)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, stop)
        deadline = time.time() + self.options.run_for if self.options.run_for else None
        while not stopping["now"]:
            started = time.time()
            try:
                self.cycle()
            except Exception as error:  # a guard that crashes must say so, not vanish
                log(f"cycle failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
            if self.options.once or (deadline and time.time() >= deadline):
                break
            time.sleep(max(0.2, self.options.interval - (time.time() - started)))
        return 0


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter
    )
    parser.add_argument("--interval", type=float, default=5.0, help="seconds between cycles")
    parser.add_argument("--warn", type=float, default=0.25, help="warn below this fraction of MemTotal")
    parser.add_argument("--shed", type=float, default=0.125, help="shed below this fraction of MemTotal")
    parser.add_argument("--hard", type=float, default=0.08, help="below this fraction, SIGKILL with no grace")
    parser.add_argument("--clear", type=float, default=0.32, help="clear above this fraction (hysteresis)")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--slice", default="user.slice/user-1000.slice",
        help="the bounded slice whose memory.max, unreclaimable headroom and events this guard reads",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--slice-warn", type=float, default=25.0, help="GiB of slice unreclaimable headroom to memory.max at which to warn")
    parser.add_argument("--slice-shed", type=float, default=12.0, help="GiB of slice unreclaimable headroom to memory.max at which to shed")
    parser.add_argument("--swap-warn", type=float, default=0.25, help="open an incident when free swap falls below this fraction")
    parser.add_argument("--grace", type=float, default=10.0, help="seconds between SIGTERM and SIGKILL")
    parser.add_argument("--shed-interval", type=float, default=20.0, help="seconds between sheds above the no-grace level")
    parser.add_argument("--cooldown", type=float, default=15.0, help="seconds after a SIGKILL before the next shed (lets MemAvailable catch up)")
    parser.add_argument("--min-victim", type=float, default=1.0, help="GiB: never shed a session smaller than this")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--protect", action="append", default=[INFRA_LEAD, CHIEF_OF_STAFF],
        help="a session id to spare until it is the only candidate (repeatable)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--session-comm", default="omp", help="the process name that marks a session (tests use their own)")
    parser.add_argument("--heartbeat", type=float, default=300.0, help="seconds between journal heartbeats while healthy")
    parser.add_argument("--state", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_STATE, help="the owned state file")
    parser.add_argument("--http-timeout", type=float, default=8.0)
    parser.add_argument("--once", action="store_true", help="one cycle, then exit")
    parser.add_argument("--run-for", type=float, default=0.0, help="seconds to run, then exit (0 = forever)")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="decide and log; send nothing, kill nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--print-alert", action="store_true", help="print the text it would send")
    options = parser.parse_args()
    # A run on any other state file than the service's own has its own memory of the incident,
    # so it must not be able to kill a real session: it may act only on a test's own process
    # name. This is what makes the proof run safe to do on the live box.
    options.test_only = options.state != DEFAULT_STATE
    if options.test_only and options.session_comm == "omp":
        log(f"--state is not {DEFAULT_STATE}: refusing to shed real omp sessions (use --session-comm)")
    return Guard(options).run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
